WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.397
Now that you can measure, we start fixing.

00:00:04.697 --> 00:00:09.923
First, a smell that almost every team building on models has, and almost nobody

00:00:09.923 --> 00:00:11.510
notices until month-end.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.340
A company extracts data from supplier contracts with two steps.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.081
The first one reads the contract and lists the main data.

00:00:20.381 --> 00:00:25.192
The second takes that text and fills in the record in the company's management

00:00:25.192 --> 00:00:25.624
system.

00:00:26.274 --> 00:00:28.573
And between the two steps, there's this.

00:00:28.873 --> 00:00:33.866
A regular expression that looks for the word Total, a colon, and a number.

00:00:34.516 --> 00:00:36.126
It worked for three months.

00:00:36.426 --> 00:00:41.475
Then one day, the model started writing total contract value, instead of just total.

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:44.075
The regular expression found nothing.

00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:45.900
The total became empty.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.176
And one hundred forty contracts went into the system with no amount, before anyone

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:51.662
noticed.

00:00:52.312 --> 00:00:53.360
Nothing broke.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.556
No exception, no alert.

00:00:55.856 --> 00:00:57.838
The data was just empty.

00:00:58.138 --> 00:01:03.320
In this lesson, you'll learn to recognize this smell, why it's so tempting, and the

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.753
one refactoring that removes the whole class of errors.

00:01:07.953 --> 00:01:09.142
Here's the shape of it.

00:01:09.442 --> 00:01:13.096
The first step produces text meant for a person to read.

00:01:13.746 --> 00:01:17.020
And that text is the only interface between the two steps.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:22.891
Code, or sometimes the next model, tries to guess where each piece of data is.

00:01:23.541 --> 00:01:25.951
When the guess is right, everything works.

00:01:26.601 --> 00:01:31.230
When the model changes how it writes, the data disappears, with no alarm.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:32.876
How do you spot it?

00:01:33.176 --> 00:01:38.600
One step returns prose, and the next step uses a regular expression, a split, or

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.701
find the line that starts with.

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:44.613
The code tolerates several names for the same field.

00:01:44.913 --> 00:01:47.334
Total, total value, grand total.

00:01:47.984 --> 00:01:53.819
The second step is another model call, whose only job is to understand the first

00:01:53.819 --> 00:01:54.767
one's format.

00:01:55.417 --> 00:01:59.364
Changing one word in a prompt breaks a parser in another file.

00:02:00.014 --> 00:02:04.198
And missing fields silently become empty, instead of raising an error.

00:02:05.398 --> 00:02:08.671
It usually starts with a prototype in a chat window.

00:02:08.971 --> 00:02:14.228
Someone tested the prompt, liked the text answer, and pasted it into the code.

00:02:14.528 --> 00:02:16.568
In a chat, text is the product.

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:19.564
In a pipeline, text is a live wire.

00:02:20.214 --> 00:02:21.674
Text feels flexible.

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:26.645
You don't have to decide names, types, or what's required before starting.

00:02:26.945 --> 00:02:28.692
A schema makes you decide.

00:02:29.342 --> 00:02:31.219
And it works, for a while.

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:35.468
Models often repeat the format, until the day they don't.

00:02:35.768 --> 00:02:39.701
The model changed, the prompt changed, or just the input did.

00:02:40.001 --> 00:02:43.333
A contract that says amount instead of total.

00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:46.829
And here's the trap for someone starting out.

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:52.703
Asking for JSON in the prompt, reply with JSON only, and thinking that settled it.

00:02:53.003 --> 00:02:54.185
That's still text.

00:02:54.485 --> 00:02:58.638
The model can wrap it in formatting, add a comma, or leave out a key.

00:02:59.838 --> 00:03:02.109
The worst cost is silent bad data.

00:03:02.409 --> 00:03:04.033
Nothing breaks.

00:03:04.333 --> 00:03:08.619
One hundred forty contracts with no total raise no exception.

00:03:08.919 --> 00:03:11.980
Finance only sees the hole at month-end close.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.248
Then there's the coupling nobody can see.

00:03:15.548 --> 00:03:20.462
The first step's prompt becomes part of the second step's contract, and nobody knows

00:03:20.462 --> 00:03:20.638
it.

00:03:20.938 --> 00:03:26.255
The parser lives in another file, another repository, maybe another team.

00:03:26.905 --> 00:03:28.106
Then fear.

00:03:28.406 --> 00:03:31.507
Any prompt tweak or model swap becomes a risk.

00:03:31.807 --> 00:03:36.608
The team stops improving the extractor, because finance's regular expression will

00:03:36.608 --> 00:03:36.964
break.

00:03:37.614 --> 00:03:38.954
And wasted tokens.

00:03:39.254 --> 00:03:43.212
Prose meant for a parser spends output on words nobody reads.

00:03:43.512 --> 00:03:47.150
The total contract value, taxes included, is.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.549
Dozens of tokens a single field doesn't need.

00:03:51.749 --> 00:03:53.144
Here's one for you.

00:03:53.444 --> 00:03:58.028
The team asked for JSON in the prompt, with the keys total and date, and the code

00:03:58.028 --> 00:03:58.594
parses it.

00:03:58.894 --> 00:04:04.773
Once a week, the run breaks on an extra comma, or a formatting fence around the

00:04:04.773 --> 00:04:05.294
object.

00:04:05.594 --> 00:04:08.542
Which next step removes the whole class of error?

00:04:11.592 --> 00:04:16.276
Use the API's structured output, with a schema, and validate the fields in code.

00:04:16.576 --> 00:04:21.883
Format stops being a request to the model, and becomes a guarantee from the API.

00:04:22.533 --> 00:04:25.809
Retrying until it parses hides the instability.

00:04:26.109 --> 00:04:31.274
And on the day the variation is a string where a number belongs, the parse succeeds,

00:04:31.274 --> 00:04:32.811
and the bad data goes in.

00:04:33.111 --> 00:04:38.143
Adding three examples of valid JSON lowers the rate, but never to zero.

00:04:38.443 --> 00:04:42.046
You'd still depend on the model obeying a format instruction.

00:04:43.246 --> 00:04:47.921
The treatment is a refactoring called replace free text with structured output.

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:51.064
The contract between the steps becomes explicit.

00:04:51.714 --> 00:04:57.600
The supplier's tax ID, the invoice's access key, which may be missing, the total,

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.653
typed as money, and the list of due dates.

00:05:01.303 --> 00:05:03.106
The API guarantees the shape.

00:05:03.406 --> 00:05:06.819
The code reads the total field, not a sentence.

00:05:07.119 --> 00:05:11.283
A phrase like total contract value simply has nowhere to go anymore.

00:05:11.583 --> 00:05:15.099
And a missing required field becomes an error right away.

00:05:15.399 --> 00:05:20.561
The document goes to human review, instead of entering the system as zero.

00:05:21.211 --> 00:05:22.867
And often there's a bonus.

00:05:23.167 --> 00:05:28.506
If the second step only existed to turn the text into fields, it can go away.

00:05:28.806 --> 00:05:31.356
The first call already returns the fields.

00:05:32.006 --> 00:05:34.573
But a schema doesn't replace an eval.

00:05:34.873 --> 00:05:39.931
A correct shape can still carry the tax ID of a company that isn't the issuer.

00:05:40.231 --> 00:05:43.122
The eval measures whether the value is right.

00:05:43.422 --> 00:05:47.726
The schema guarantees the value arrived as a value.

00:05:48.926 --> 00:05:50.268
When can you ignore it?

00:05:50.568 --> 00:05:52.839
When the destination is a person.

00:05:53.139 --> 00:05:56.949
A chat reply or an email to a customer should be text.

00:05:57.249 --> 00:05:59.650
The smell is text between machines.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.888
When the text is the product.

00:06:02.188 --> 00:06:05.460
One step writes an article draft, and another reviews it.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.462
Passing text between them is perfectly legitimate.

00:06:09.762 --> 00:06:15.023
Structure what code actually uses, like the list of problems found, or whether it

00:06:15.023 --> 00:06:15.867
was approved.

00:06:16.167 --> 00:06:17.641
Not the article itself.

00:06:18.291 --> 00:06:20.483
And a throwaway prototype is fine.

00:06:20.783 --> 00:06:25.754
The day the parser goes into the real system, the schema goes in with it.

00:06:26.404 --> 00:06:28.648
One last case, to sharpen the eye.

00:06:28.948 --> 00:06:34.099
A ticket classifier answers with a single word, billing, order or other, and code

00:06:34.099 --> 00:06:35.244
compares the text.

00:06:35.544 --> 00:06:40.362
Sometimes it returns billing with a capital letter and a period, and the ticket

00:06:40.362 --> 00:06:41.886
lands in the wrong queue.

00:06:42.186 --> 00:06:43.299
Is that this smell?

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:44.249
Yes.

00:06:44.549 --> 00:06:49.299
Even one word is free text, if your code depends on its exact form.

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:54.624
A schema with a closed list of values fixes it, with just one field.

00:06:55.824 --> 00:06:57.809
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:06:58.109 --> 00:06:59.404
Text is for people.

00:06:59.704 --> 00:07:04.819
When one step hands data to code, or to another step, the contract has to be a

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:06.327
schema, not a sentence.

00:07:06.627 --> 00:07:11.666
Otherwise your system works until the day the model finds a nicer way to say total.

00:07:12.316 --> 00:07:17.296
In the next lesson, we'll do the refactoring itself, step by step, with an eval

00:07:17.296 --> 00:07:21.015
before and after: replace free text with structured output.

00:07:21.315 --> 00:07:22.145
See you there.
